      "18258": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "is_private": false,
            "time": "2021-12-19T20:48:16Z",
            "id": 98235,
            "tags": [],
            "attachment_id": {},
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "Create a package named 'testTrace' with only two functions:\n```\nfunctionFactory <- function() {\n  return(function(...) print(...))\n}\n\ngeneratedF <- functionFactory()\n```\ngeneratedF is non-standard in the fact that `parent.env(environment(generatedF)` is *not* the package namespace - and it seems that methods::.TraceWithMethods relies on it.  So tracing `generatedF` is proving to be problematic.\n\nConsider two ways of invoking `generatedF`:\n(1) `generatedF(3)`\n(2) `get(\"generatedF\", asNamespace(\"testTrace\"))(3)`    \n   (I believe 2 is essentially as dependent packages invoke it)\n\nConsider also 3 ways of setting a trace:\nA. trace(generatedF, tracer=function() print(\"1\"))\n This causes (1) to be traced, but not (2).\nB. trace(generatedF, tracer=function() print(\"1\"), where=asNamespace(\"testTrace\"))\nC. trace(generatedF, tracer=function() print(\"1\"), where=functionFactory)\n These cause (2) to be traced, but not (1).",
            "bug_id": 18258,
            "creator": "ofekshilon",
            "creation_time": "2021-12-19T20:48:16Z"
          },
          {
            "tags": [],
            "is_private": false,
            "id": 102154,
            "creation_time": "2025-09-02T10:00:06Z",
            "count": 1,
            "bug_id": 18258,
            "text": "I can confirm that this is present in R-devel (as of yesterday). Patch and test code attached.\n\nThe issue is actually with `environment(generatedF)` (which is not the namespace) rather than `parent.env(environment(generatedF))` (which is the namespace).\n\nThe culprit ultimately lies in `methods:::.assignOverBinding`. This is what is used to assign the new `functionWithTrace` object over the original object when `trace` is invoked with a tracer argument (or any argument beyond what, in fact). `.assignOverBinding` contains:\n\n```\nfunction (what, value, where, verbose = TRUE) \n{\n <snip>\n     if (is.function(value)) {\n        fenv <- environment(value)\n        if (is.null(fenv)) \n            fenv <- baseenv()\n        if (!identical(fenv, where) && exists(what, envir = fenv, \n            inherits = FALSE) && bindingIsLocked(what, fenv)) {\n            unlockBinding(what, fenv)\n            assign(what, value, fenv)\n            lockBinding(what, fenv)\n        }\n    }\n    if (exists(what, envir = where, inherits = FALSE) && bindingIsLocked(what, \n        where)) {\n        unlockBinding(what, where)\n        assign(what, value, where)\n        lockBinding(what, where)\n    }\n    else assign(what, value, where)\n}\n```\n\nFor \"standard\" package functions, e.g., `functionFactory`, `fenv` is the namespace while `where` will be the package environment, and the namespace has a binding for the symbol, so \n\n```\n!identical(fenv, where) && exists(what, envir = fenv, \n            inherits = FALSE) && bindingIsLocked(what, fenv)\n```\nevaluates to `TRUE`, thus unlocking, assignment into, and locking of `fenv` (again, the package namespace) occurs.\n\nWith `generatedF`, however, `fenv` is a call frame from when `functionFactory` was called to create it. `fenv` is again not equal to `where` (pkg environment) but `fenv` *does not have a binding for the symbol* so the condition above evaluates to `FALSE`. This means that the traced function object is assigned into the pkg environment (`where`) but NOT into `fenv` **or** the package namespace.\n\n`.assignOverBinding` is used in a couple of other contexts, so my proposed patch leaves the default behavior as is but adds a `findFunBinding` argument, defaulting to `FALSE` that, if `TRUE`, walks up the parent chain of `fenv` until it finds an environment with a binding for `what` (the function name being traced) and uses that as `fenv` for purposes of the condition and subsequent conditional assignment. This fixes the inconsistent behavior between tracing `generatedF` and `functionFactory`. I have added specific regression tests and have attached a wide test suite in the form of a script.",
            "attachment_id": {},
            "time": "2025-09-02T10:00:06Z",
            "creator": "gabembecker",
            "is_markdown": true
          },
          {
